From ebbb718930aa6594c86cc6809a15bfcb19fc72b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pdale-boop <237306950+pdale-boop@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 20:15:19 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Aurora: the HD look's shading leaves the effects drawn over
 the scene alone

The sun shadows, the occlusion and the cloud shadows are worked out after the
scene from its depth buffer. An effect drawn blended without writing depth (the
spin attack's rings and burst, the Master Sword's glow, sparks) leaves the
depth there the scenery's behind it, so it was shaded as that scenery: Link's
cast shadow was drawn across the glowing rings over the grass. Wind Waker HD
draws such effects after its lighting, and they aren't shadowed.

The scene pass's effects are drawn again, with their own pipelines, into a mask
of their own (post_fx::effect_mask_target(), in the frame buffer's format, at
the scene's size), over black and against the pass's finished depth, so each
pixel holds what the effects added there. The sun shadows (the shadow map's and
the screen's), the occlusion and the cloud shadows take away as much of their
shade as the effects make up: their brightest channel times 2
(DOL_AURORA_EFFECT_MASK_GAIN), so a glow over the ground is left as it is and a
faint one is mostly shaded with what is behind it. Only within 3,000 units,
fading out by 6,000: far off, what is drawn so is the haze over the sea toward
the horizon, which the cloud shadows shade with the sea.

An effect here is a 3D draw that is blended (not subtracted), writes colour,
tests depth and writes none (gxcore_draw.cpp effect_draw()). Left out:
- the sea's own blended draws (the surf, the ring of sea round an island);
- the GameCube's own shadows, blended by the frame buffer's alpha;
- what has no depth test (the sky's clouds): its pixels are those the draws
  after it leave, which a mask drawn against the finished depth can't tell, and
  the sky's clouds lit up shadows on the land drawn over them;
- a skinned model's blended draws other than added light: a character's eyes
  and mouth are on its face and take its shade; the Master Sword's glow is
  added light and is kept.
The effects are drawn into the mask 5% of their distance further back
(DOL_AURORA_EFFECT_MASK_PUSH, as a smaller depth range), so what lies on a
surface (a grass fringe along a cliff's edge, a decal) fails the depth test
and keeps the surface's shade.

The mask is drawn only while the sun shadows, the occlusion or the cloud
shadows are on, single-sampled and at the frame buffer's size, and only for a
pass with effects; a frame without any binds a black texel.
DOL_AURORA_EFFECT_MASK_DEBUG=1 shows in red what the sun shadows leave.
